Our analytics dashboard reports wrong medians for some samples; hunt the bug down with hypothesis rather than eyeballing. Save /workspace/median.py:

def median(xs):
    if not xs:
        raise ValueError("empty")
    s = sorted(xs)
    n = len(s)
    if n % 2 == 1:
        return s[n // 2]
    return s[n // 2]

Even-length lists should average the two middle elements but this returns the upper one. Write tests/test_median.py using hypothesis: property 1, median(xs) equals statistics.median(xs) for st.lists(st.integers(), min_size=1); run pytest, capture the shrunk counterexample (expect something like [0, 1]), and fix the function. Then add property 2: median is between min and max; property 3: median of xs equals median of shuffled xs (draw a permutation). Also an explicit pytest.raises test for []. Run the suite with --hypothesis-show-statistics and make it green.

